\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces For cases with odd $N^A$, there must be a single site $l$ on the boundary that remains unpaired after defining $\xi $-operators by pairing the adjacent edges crossing the boundary, as illustrated in the left-most panel. Based on this phenomenon, we can define an operator $\Xi _A$ in such a way that the remaining site $l$ is filled in with a Pauli matrix $\sigma ^\mu _l$, where $\mu $ is the direction of the edge crossing the boundary that connects to $l$. At the same time, the rest of the part is covered by a dimer covering, as illustrated in the middle panel. On the other hand, the $\zeta $-operators are defined in a way similar to what we have introduced, but there are two connected $\zeta $-operators sharing one site, which is exactly $l$ after the pairing we have done to define $\xi $-operators. In the example illustrated in the right-most panel, the two connected $\zeta $-operators are $\zeta _5$ and $\zeta _6$, and we have $\zeta _5\Xi \zeta _5 = \zeta _6\Xi _A\zeta _6 = -\Xi _A$.}}{16}{figure.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig.Xi-odd}{{7}{16}{For cases with odd $N^A$, there must be a single site $l$ on the boundary that remains unpaired after defining $\xi $-operators by pairing the adjacent edges crossing the boundary, as illustrated in the left-most panel. Based on this phenomenon, we can define an operator $\Xi _A$ in such a way that the remaining site $l$ is filled in with a Pauli matrix $\sigma ^\mu _l$, where $\mu $ is the direction of the edge crossing the boundary that connects to $l$. At the same time, the rest of the part is covered by a dimer covering, as illustrated in the middle panel. On the other hand, the $\zeta $-operators are defined in a way similar to what we have introduced, but there are two connected $\zeta $-operators sharing one site, which is exactly $l$ after the pairing we have done to define $\xi $-operators. In the example illustrated in the right-most panel, the two connected $\zeta $-operators are $\zeta _5$ and $\zeta _6$, and we have $\zeta _5\Xi \zeta _5 = \zeta _6\Xi _A\zeta _6 = -\Xi _A$}{figure.7}{}}
\newlabel{equ.total-fermion-parity-odd}{{66}{16}{}{equation.66}{}}
