\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces For regions with an even number of lattice sites, at least a dimer covering is well defined, like the one illustrated in the right panel. Assigning a string operator to each dimer in the configuration gives a dimer-covering string operator $\Xi _A$ without a single unpaired site, unlike we encountered in cases with an odd number of sites. However, it can be directly checked that $\Xi _A$ in this case commutes with every $\zeta $-operator. Therefore, such an operator doesn't provide additional degeneracy in the entanglement spectrum.}}{17}{figure.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig.dimer-covering}{{8}{17}{For regions with an even number of lattice sites, at least a dimer covering is well defined, like the one illustrated in the right panel. Assigning a string operator to each dimer in the configuration gives a dimer-covering string operator $\Xi _A$ without a single unpaired site, unlike we encountered in cases with an odd number of sites. However, it can be directly checked that $\Xi _A$ in this case commutes with every $\zeta $-operator. Therefore, such an operator doesn't provide additional degeneracy in the entanglement spectrum}{figure.8}{}}
