\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces A triangle lattice and a honeycomb lattice are dual to each other, in the sense that each lattice point of a system is at the center of a face of the other one. The boundary $C=\partial A$ of the shaded region $A$ is a \textit  {circuit} defined in the triangle lattice. As shown above, $A$ is composed of $f$ faces of the triangular lattice, in which each face corresponds to a vertex of the dual honeycomb lattice, indicating $f=N^A$. The darker shaded region is composed of $N^A_p$ hexagons entirely inside $A$, which is identical to counting the number of vertices $v$ of the triangular lattice circled by $C$. The length $\ell \equiv \abs {C}$ of the circuit can be understood as the number of edges in the honeycomb lattice that cross $C$. }}{20}{figure.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig.dual-lattice-Pick-theorem}{{9}{20}{A triangle lattice and a honeycomb lattice are dual to each other, in the sense that each lattice point of a system is at the center of a face of the other one. The boundary $C=\de A$ of the shaded region $A$ is a \textit {circuit} defined in the triangle lattice. As shown above, $A$ is composed of $f$ faces of the triangular lattice, in which each face corresponds to a vertex of the dual honeycomb lattice, indicating $f=N^A$. The darker shaded region is composed of $N^A_p$ hexagons entirely inside $A$, which is identical to counting the number of vertices $v$ of the triangular lattice circled by $C$. The length $\ell \equiv \abs {C}$ of the circuit can be understood as the number of edges in the honeycomb lattice that cross $C$}{figure.9}{}}
